Subject: Re: [r-inla] INLA prediction formula
Message-ID: <uFyUQ5xWk5M.wSFL2JZLdFYJ@r-inla-discussion-group.groups.google.com>
In-Reply-To: <uFyUQ5xWk5M.l48gMG6H8kUJ@r-inla-discussion-group.groups.google.com>
References: <uFyUQ5xWk5M.2iOKQeIiJPcJ@r-inla-discussion-group.groups.google.com> <uFyUQ5xWk5M.l48gMG6H8kUJ@r-inla-discussion-group.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/r-inla-discussion-group/c/uFyUQ5xWk5M/m/wSFL2JZLdFYJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The fitted.values distributions depend on the link function.  You need to specify that the link function should be applied to all predictions, and not only to the ones with observed data (by default it's applied only to non-NA data lines)
Please se the FAQ about predictions on r-inla.org <http://r-inla.org>.

Also, so you need to be very careful about comparing point estimates; E(exp(x)) is not the same thing as exp(E(x)), for example.  Instead, transform the entire distributions, if needed.

Finn

